1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,184
ZERO needs one thing from the supermarket.

2
00:00:03,484 --> 00:00:04,300
Milk.

3
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:15,527
The milk is the farthest

4
00:00:15,527 --> 00:00:16,716
thing from the door.

5
00:00:17,016 --> 00:00:18,888
Who decided that?

6
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:32,411
Two explanations are fighting

7
00:00:32,411 --> 00:00:33,502
over this carton.

8
00:00:33,802 --> 00:00:35,674
One has a villain.

9
00:00:36,174 --> 00:00:38,046
The other has a truck.

10
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,046
Behind every dairy wall, there

11
00:00:43,046 --> 00:00:44,544
is a room like this.

12
00:00:44,844 --> 00:00:46,716
Cold on purpose.

13
00:00:47,116 --> 00:00:48,988
Three degrees.

14
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,368
For milk, cold is not a preference.

15
00:00:53,668 --> 00:00:56,482
The rule says: cooled to seven degrees

16
00:00:56,482 --> 00:00:58,852
Celsius or less, and kept there.

17
00:00:59,252 --> 00:01:00,980
So what happens when it isn’t?

18
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:05,436
One small experiment measured it.

19
00:01:05,736 --> 00:01:08,112
About three hours at room temperature,

20
00:01:08,412 --> 00:01:10,312
and a carton loses a whole

21
00:01:10,312 --> 00:01:11,628
day of shelf life.

22
00:01:11,828 --> 00:01:14,005
So how does a store keep it cold, all

23
00:01:14,005 --> 00:01:15,476
the way from the factory?

24
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,048
It arrives in a refrigerated truck.

25
00:01:20,348 --> 00:01:22,388
The truck backs up to a dock.

26
00:01:22,788 --> 00:01:25,092
And the dock is at the back of the store.

27
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,924
Truck. Dock. Cold room. Dairy case.

28
00:01:35,224 --> 00:01:37,430
A Wegmans manager told NPR

29
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:38,872
why that matters:

30
00:01:39,372 --> 00:01:41,253
“We don’t have to worry about ever

31
00:01:41,253 --> 00:01:42,636
breaking the cold chain.”

32
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:48,340
Now put the milk at the front door.

33
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:50,997
Every carton would cross the whole

34
00:01:50,997 --> 00:01:53,008
store, past the warm shelves.

35
00:01:53,408 --> 00:01:55,732
Ten minutes out of the cold costs

36
00:01:55,732 --> 00:01:57,704
about an hour of shelf life.

37
00:01:58,104 --> 00:01:59,976
So is it just plumbing?

38
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,372
But look at ZERO’s receipt.

39
00:02:05,672 --> 00:02:07,112
He came for one thing.

40
00:02:07,512 --> 00:02:09,384
He is leaving with four.

41
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,254
That is the other explanation:

42
00:02:17,254 --> 00:02:18,304
the walk-through.

43
00:02:18,604 --> 00:02:20,442
Put the milk at the back, and

44
00:02:20,442 --> 00:02:21,964
people pass the shelves.

45
00:02:22,364 --> 00:02:23,770
Some of them buy things

46
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:24,932
they never planned.

47
00:02:25,332 --> 00:02:27,204
But how many?

48
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,913
The average American supermarket carries

49
00:02:34,913 --> 00:02:36,672
thirty-three thousand items.

50
00:02:36,972 --> 00:02:38,263
One of them has to sit

51
00:02:38,263 --> 00:02:39,612
farthest from the door.

52
00:02:40,012 --> 00:02:42,076
And the store gets to choose which.

53
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:48,356
For decades, one number kept

54
00:02:48,356 --> 00:02:49,544
getting repeated.

55
00:02:49,844 --> 00:02:51,798
Sixty to seventy percent of

56
00:02:51,798 --> 00:02:54,404
supermarket purchases are unplanned.

57
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:00,247
A 1999 study followed thirty thousand

58
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:02,556
purchases in fourteen cities.

59
00:03:02,856 --> 00:03:05,376
On big trips, sixty-eight percent of the

60
00:03:05,376 --> 00:03:07,392
items were decided in the store.

61
00:03:07,792 --> 00:03:10,168
On small trips, fifty-four.

62
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:14,926
A 2008 study in Dutch supermarkets

63
00:03:14,926 --> 00:03:16,548
asked it another way.

64
00:03:16,848 --> 00:03:19,128
For each category in the basket:

65
00:03:19,128 --> 00:03:21,480
planned, or decided in the store?

66
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:23,752
Eighteen percent.

67
00:03:24,252 --> 00:03:26,484
About one category per trip.

68
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:32,444
Same question. Sixty-eight and eighteen.

69
00:03:32,744 --> 00:03:35,098
They count different things: items

70
00:03:35,098 --> 00:03:37,452
against categories, American trips

71
00:03:37,452 --> 00:03:38,768
against Dutch ones.

72
00:03:39,168 --> 00:03:42,000
Both are real. Neither is the whole story.

73
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:46,964
And do shoppers even walk past everything?

74
00:03:47,264 --> 00:03:49,635
Researchers tracked thousands of

75
00:03:49,635 --> 00:03:51,488
carts in one supermarket.

76
00:03:51,888 --> 00:03:53,832
Not one long trip went down

77
00:03:53,832 --> 00:03:55,128
all twelve aisles.

78
00:03:55,528 --> 00:03:57,802
Most people use the outer loop as home

79
00:03:57,802 --> 00:04:00,256
base, and dart into the aisles they need.

80
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,172
So here is the whole picture.

81
00:04:07,472 --> 00:04:09,849
A reason put the milk there: the

82
00:04:09,849 --> 00:04:12,152
truck, the cold room, the rule.

83
00:04:12,552 --> 00:04:15,245
A benefit keeps it there: whoever walks to

84
00:04:15,245 --> 00:04:17,232
the milk passes something else.

85
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:28,656
Even the stores give both answers.

86
00:04:28,956 --> 00:04:31,666
A Kroger spokesperson told NPR the cold

87
00:04:31,666 --> 00:04:33,612
chain, and the walk-through.

88
00:04:34,012 --> 00:04:37,036
A BJ’s dairy buyer said the same: the cold

89
00:04:37,036 --> 00:04:39,412
chain, and “building the basket.”

90
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,820
A supermarket consultant told NPR

91
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:48,240
the milk has been in the back for

92
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:50,000
seventy to eighty years.

93
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:52,172
He would move it to the front.

94
00:04:52,572 --> 00:04:55,308
In most stores, it is still back there.

95
00:04:57,400 --> 00:04:59,824
What does that mean for ZERO’s basket?

96
00:05:00,124 --> 00:05:02,457
In the Dutch study, shoppers who called

97
00:05:02,457 --> 00:05:04,611
themselves fast and efficient bought

98
00:05:04,611 --> 00:05:06,944
eighty-two percent less on impulse than

99
00:05:06,944 --> 00:05:08,140
the average shopper.

100
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:11,015
Trips for something needed right away:

101
00:05:11,015 --> 00:05:12,644
fifty-three percent less.

102
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:21,208
Next week, ZERO goes for milk again.

103
00:05:21,508 --> 00:05:23,476
This time, he knows the way.

104
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:33,033
Some of what you buy was

105
00:05:33,033 --> 00:05:34,168
never on your list.

106
00:05:34,468 --> 00:05:35,865
And some of what was on it

107
00:05:35,865 --> 00:05:36,940
never makes it home.
